<article class="env-lemma" id="0E22">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0E22">Lemma <span data-tag="0E22">33.44.13</span>.</a> Let $k$ be a field. Let $X$ be a proper scheme over $k$ which is reduced, connected, and equidimensional of dimension $1$. Let $\mathcal{L}$ be an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module. If $\deg (\mathcal{L}|_ C) \leq 0$ for all irreducible components $C$ of $X$, then either $H^0(X, \mathcal{L}) = 0$ or $\mathcal{L} \cong \mathcal{O}_ X$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $s \in H^0(X, \mathcal{L})$ be nonzero. Since $X$ is reduced there exists an irreducible component $C$ of $X$ with $s|_ C \not= 0$. But if $s|_ C$ is nonzero, then $s$ is nonwhere vanishing on $C$ by Lemma <a href="/tag/0B40" data-tag="0B40">33.44.12</a>. This in turn implies $s$ is nowhere vanishing on every irreducible component of $X$ meeting $C$. Since $X$ is connected, we conclude that $s$ vanishes nowhere and the lemma follows. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>